.TH SIMP 1 "2026" "simp 0.0.1" "User Commands"
.SH NAME
simp \- compiler for the Simple programming language
.SH SYNOPSIS
.B simp
[\fIoptions\fR] \fIinput\fR...
.br
.B simp
\-\-print\-paths [\fB\-M\fR \fIdir\fR] [\fIinput\fR...]
.SH DESCRIPTION
.B simp
compiles Simple source files
.RI ( .simp )
to LLVM IR and invokes
.BR clang (1)
to produce an executable, or a relocatable object with
.BR \-c .
Object inputs
.RI ( .o ,
.IR .obj )
are linked with the generated code. Exactly one source input must contain the
top\-level
.B start
block when an executable is produced.
.PP
The compiler is relocatable: it locates its runtime library, runtime headers,
String builtin, and standard modules relative to its own executable, so an
installed tree may be moved as a unit.
.SH OPTIONS
.TP
.BR \-h ", " \-\-help
Print option help and exit.
.TP
.BR \-V ", " \-\-version
Print the compiler version and exit.
.TP
.B \-v
Increase verbosity; may be repeated or grouped
.RB ( \-vv ,
.BR \-vvv ).
Level 1 reports compiler phases, level 2 also prints every resolved path and
the exact
.B clang
commands, and level 3 adds per\-phase timings. All verbose output goes to
standard error.
.TP
.BI \-\-verbosity= N
Set the verbosity level directly (0 to 3). It cannot be combined with
.BR \-v .
Levels above 3 are rejected.
.TP
.BR \-t ", " \-\-trace =\fItarget\fR[,\fItarget\fR...]
Trace compiler internals. Targets are
.BR scanner ,
.BR parser ,
.BR ast ,
and
.BR symbols ;
they are case\-insensitive and may be given as a comma\-separated list or by
repeating the option. Scanner and parser traces go to standard error; the AST
and symbol table are printed to standard output.
.TP
.B \-\-print\-paths
Print the resolved executable, prefix, runtime directory and library, include
directory, builtin, project root, project module root and its source, standard
module directory, deprecated compatibility roots, module registry, and the
.B clang
executable, then exit successfully. No input is required.
.TP
.B \-\-check\-only
Parse and check the source inputs without generating code.
.TP
.BR \-c ", " \-\-compile\-only
Compile the source inputs to one relocatable object without linking.
.TP
.BR \-g ", " \-\-debug
Emit DWARF debug information.
.TP
.BR \-o ", " \-\-output " \fIfile\fR"
Write the executable or object to
.IR file .
.TP
.BI \-\-emit\-llvm " file"
Also save the generated LLVM IR to
.IR file .
.TP
.BR \-p ", " \-\-path " \fIdir\fR[:\fIdir\fR...]"
Add directories to the textual include search path.
.TP
.BI \-\-max\-include\-depth " n"
Limit nested textual includes (default 16).
.TP
.BR \-M ", " \-\-module\-dir " \fIdir\fR"
Use
.I dir
as the project module root. The directory must exist.
.TP
.BI \-\-package\-path " dir\fR[:\fIdir\fR...]"
Deprecated. Add compatibility package roots searched after the project and
standard module roots. A warning is printed when used.
.TP
.BR \-L ", " \-\-library\-path " \fIdir\fR"
Add a linker search directory.
.TP
.BR \-l ", " \-\-library " \fIname\fR"
Link with library
.IR name .
.SH MODULES
An
.B import
resolves a package from
.IR root / name / version /simp\-package.toml .
Module roots are searched in this order:
.IP 1. 4
The project module root:
.B \-M
if given, otherwise
.BR SIMP_MODULE_DIR ,
otherwise
.IR project\-root /modules ,
where the project root is the nearest source ancestor containing simpkg.toml
or a modules directory (starting at the current directory without source input).
Without a marker, the source parent is used. A missing default root
is not an error.
.IP 2. 4
The standard module directory shipped with the compiler.
.IP 3. 4
Deprecated compatibility roots from
.B \-\-package\-path
and then
.BR SIMP_PACKAGE_PATH .
.IP 4. 4
The deprecated tab\-separated registry
.RB ( SIMP_MODULE_REGISTRY ,
default
.IR ./simp\-modules.tsv ).
A warning is printed whenever a module is resolved through it. Package
manifests take precedence over registry entries.
.PP
The first module root containing a package shadows lower\-priority roots. Without
a project selection policy, the highest stable version is selected only from
the versions in that root.
.PP
New projects use simpkg.toml for direct dependencies and simpkg.lock for the
complete exact graph. Selecting such a project's modules directory uses the
adjacent lock, including when selected explicitly with \-M or SIMP_MODULE_DIR.
Selecting another directory instead uses that directory's policy.
The compiler checks the lock schema, manifest freshness, selected package
contents, and graph edges without network access. Missing or invalid locks
are errors. Combining simpkg.toml with modules/modules.toml is rejected.
Use simpkg init, simpkg add OWNER/REPO \-\-yes, and simpkg install \-\-yes;
ordinary compilation does not require evaluating simpkg env.
.PP
For legacy projects without simpkg.toml, an optional
.I modules.toml
in the canonical project module root (selected by
.BR \-M ,
.BR SIMP_MODULE_DIR ,
or the default
.IR project\-root /modules )
contains a strict package allowlist and ordered exact-version preferences:
.nf
[modules]
geometry = ["1.2.3", "1.1.0"]
system = ["0.1.0"]
.fi
.PP
Each entry must be a nonempty, single-line array of distinct exact SemVer
strings; only the [modules] table is supported. The first configured version
installed in the highest-priority root is selected;
a later version is tried only when an earlier one is absent. Unlisted direct
and transitive packages are not found, including standard modules unless
listed. Exact dependency pins remain constraints and conflict rather than
causing fallback. Malformed installed packages also fail without fallback.
Legacy registry-only modules are unsupported while the policy is active.
When neither simpkg.toml nor
.I modules.toml
is present, existing version-selection and registry behavior is retained.
.PP
An unaliased import binds the manifest's declared export name; import system
exposes System.Process. An explicit import system as Sys exposes Sys.Process
instead. Colliding bindings are errors. String remains an implicit prelude.
.PP
When a module cannot be found, the diagnostic lists every module root and
registry searched.
.SH ENVIRONMENT
.TP
.B SIMP_MODULE_DIR
Project module root when
.B \-M
is not given.
.TP
.B modules.toml
Optional strict module allowlist and ordered version policy at the selected
project module root.
.TP
.B SIMP_HOME
Installation prefix used instead of the executable location.
.TP
.BR SIMP_RUNTIME_DIR ", " SIMP_INCLUDE_DIR ", " SIMP_BUILTIN_DIR ", " SIMP_STDLIB_MODULE_DIR
Override individual resource directories; each takes precedence over
.BR SIMP_HOME .
.TP
.B CC
C compiler driver used instead of the configured
.BR clang .
.TP
.BR SIMP_PACKAGE_PATH ", " SIMP_MODULE_REGISTRY
Deprecated compatibility module sources; see
.BR MODULES .
.SH FILES
Paths are relative to the installation prefix (the parent of the
.I bin
directory holding
.BR simp ).
.TP
.I bin/simp
The compiler.
.TP
.I lib/simp/libsimp_runtime.a
The garbage\-collected runtime linked into every executable.
.TP
.I include/simp/
Runtime C headers used by inline C shims.
.TP
.I share/simp/builtin/String.simp
The String builtin.
.TP
.I share/simp/modules/
Standard modules.
.SH EXIT STATUS
0 on success (including \-\-help, \-\-version, and \-\-print\-paths);
non\-zero on failure. Invalid command lines and unreadable inputs exit with 2.
.SH EXAMPLES
.nf
simp hello.simp
simp \-vv \-M ./deps app/main.simp \-o app
simp \-t parser,symbols \-\-check\-only main.simp
simp \-\-print\-paths
.fi
.SH SEE ALSO
.BR clang (1)
